    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3887518ad000f4f6b6ba083e08292dc500a63bf04ebd94be5a85c51d6d060dc2",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The product-shift averaging first turns the logarithmic phase into a polynomial that is separately structured in x and y. That separability is then exploited by repeated Hölder duplication and changes of summation order: sparse moment-curve vectors are replaced by power-sum-difference counts and finally by coordinatewise linear exponential sums, to which the rational-spacing estimate developed in the toy bilinear problem applies.",
